+
+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+The :func:`~rich.progress.open` function reads a file and shows a progress bar for the bytes consumed. The total defaults to the size of the file in bytes. In the default text mode ``'r'`` (or ``'rt'``) it returns text; pass ``'rb'`` to get bytes::
+
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("data.txt") as text_file:
+        text = text_file.read()
+
+    with open("data.bin", "rb") as binary_file:
+        data = binary_file.read()
+
+To show progress for a file as part of a larger display, use :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.open` on an existing :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance. The display is not started by this method, so use the ``Progress`` as a context manager around the read::
+
+    from rich.progress import Progress
+
+    with Progress() as progress:
+        with progress.open("data.bin", "rb", description="Reading data") as binary_file:
+            data = binary_file.read()
+
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+To track progress while reading from a binary file object you already have open, use :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file` or :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file`. Only binary file objects are supported. A total byte count is required, and the reader does not close the wrapped file, so the caller still closes it::
+
+    import os
+    from rich.progress import Progress
+
+    with Progress() as progress:
+        with open("data.bin", "rb") as raw:
+            total = os.path.getsize("data.bin")
+            with progress.wrap_file(raw, total, description="Reading data") as binary_file:
+                data = binary_file.read()
+
+With :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file`, you can instead pass an existing task with ``task_id``; if that task already has a total, no ``total`` argument is needed. The module-level :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file` always requires ``total``.
+
 Multiple Progress
 -----------------
 
